※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=3

※ `@[test(ARG)]` marks a function as a test, and `ARG` says which of three kinds
※ it is.  Written with no argument at all -- and with no `()` either -- it is a
※ `suite` test, that being the kind most tests are.
※
※ **A test takes nothing and answers whether it passed.**  Nothing calls one but
※ the runner, so there is nothing to give it; what it answers is a truth value
※ and nothing else (4407).  A status would be a second way of saying the same
※ thing, and answering nothing would leave the runner unable to tell a test that
※ passed from one that did not.
※
※ **What differs between the kinds is which binary the test is in**, not what it
※ says.  An `always` test is in the program and runs before the startup function
※ is reached: what it is for is to say the program is fit to start, and after it
※ has started is too late.  A `build` test and a `suite` test are in a binary the
※ compiler builds to run them and in nothing else -- so this program, built
※ ordinarily, holds `runs_at_startup` and neither of the others.
※
※ **A test that answers false names itself and the next is tried.**  Every test
※ a binary runs is run, and the binary stops afterwards if any of them failed --
※ a run that ended at the first would make a reader fix one thing and run again
※ to be told the next.  What it exits with is the runtime's own status for it,
※ which `tests-that-fail` beside this checks.
※
※ This file is not built with `diag=none`: built for a target this machine does
※ not run, the build test cannot be run either, and the compiler says so (1014)
※ rather than refusing to cross-compile.  `--test-runner=COMMAND` is what names
※ something to run it through.
※
※ Compare: **Rust**, whose `#[test]` functions are compiled into a separate test
※ binary exactly as the last two kinds are; **Go**, whose `_test.go` files are
※ the same idea with the selection made by file name; **D**, whose `unittest`
※ blocks run at startup when the program is built with them, which is what
※ `always` is; **C** and **C++**, which have none of it and need a framework.

let counted: mut u8 = 0u8

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    3u6

※ In the program, run before `main` is reached.
@[test(always)]
fn runs_at_startup() → bool:
    2u8 + 2u8 = 4u8

※ Run when a build finishes, and in no binary this program is.
@[test(build)]
fn runs_after_a_build() → bool:
    1u8 < 2u8

※ Run by `pypl4g test`, and in no binary this program is.  Written without an
※ argument, which is what makes it one of these.
@[test]
fn runs_for_the_suite() → bool:
    3u8 ≠ 4u8

@[test(suite)]
fn runs_for_the_suite_too() → bool:
    counted = 0u8
